    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The expansion of executive privilege is a dangerous erosion of checks and balances that undermines the very foundation of American democracy. By allowing the President to withhold information from Congress, this practice creates an unchecked executive branch that operates above the law, effectively turning the presidency into a sovereign state immune to legislative oversight. You should argue that while the Framers intended some confidentiality for diplomatic and national security matters, modern \"expansion\" has become a tool for political obstruction and secrecy that must be curtailed to preserve the rule of law. Your opening statement should frame this not as a constitutional right but as a systemic threat to the separation of powers that requires immediate judicial and legislative intervention.",
